() Tj
T*
(4. Engineering interpretation) Tj
T*
(Presence of fluid chemistry, long-range well history, ramp-up map,) Tj
T*
(and dedicated failure report indicates a system-level event) Tj
T*
(with high probability of external \(well-condition-driven\) factors.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5. Main root-cause hypothesis \(priority\)) Tj
T*
(5.1 Most probable root cause: degradation of well-fluid conditions) Tj
T*
(\(gas, water cut, solids, scaling/emulsion effects\), leading to off-design ESP operation.) Tj
T*
(5.2 Mechanism: hydraulic mismatch -> stage overloading -> accelerated wear) Tj
T*
(of rotating/support elements -> failure shutdown.) Tj
T*
(5.3 Supporting argument: chemistry data is typically central when medium effects) Tj
T*
(significantly impact ESP lifetime.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6. Alternative hypotheses) Tj
T*
(A\) Power quality disturbances \(voltage dips, phase imbalance, unstable VFD behavior\).) Tj
T*
(B\) Equipment sizing/selection mismatch versus actual operating envelope.) Tj
T*
(C\) Early manufacturing defect if operation remained inside allowable map.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(7. Operator fault assessment) Tj
T*
(7.1 Failure itself does not prove operator misconduct.) Tj
ET
